g. Opus 5.5 lost 3.2 points per move with 5 blunders; the 10k lost 3.0 with 6. Moves 50 to 53 were the sharpest stretch: Opus 5.5 gave away 11.5 points twice, and the 10k handed 11.5 back each time. The game settled at +2.2 for Black and ended with two passes.", "moments": [{"turn": 17, "text": "The 10k's F6 took Black's lead from +0.5 to -5.6, a 6.2-point loss and the worst of Black's early moves. The engine wanted B5 there. White briefly held a real edge."}, {"turn": 20, "text": "Opus 5.5 gave it straight back. J3 moved the lead from -5.7 to +0.0, a 5.7-point loss, while the engine's choice was B5. The game was level again."}, {"turn": 30, "text": "In the long fight along the top and right, the lead flipped on nearly every move. Opus 5.5's H7 took it from -0.0 to +6.5, a 6.5-point loss, and the 10k's J7 returned it from +6.5 to -0.0, losing 6.5 in turn. Both sides kept losing about as much as they gained."}, {"turn": 50, "text": "C7 was one of Opus 5.5's two 11.5-point losses, lifting Black's lead from +2.3 to +13.7 when the engine preferred B3. The 10k's D9 answered by dropping the lead back to +2.3, an 11.5-point loss of its own. The same exchange repeated at turns 52 and 53 with B9 and C9."}, {"turn": 54, "text": "Opus 5.5 finally played B3, the engine's choice, losing nothing as the lead went from +2.3 to +2.2. By then the swings had stopped, and a lead of about two points for Black held through the passes."}]}, "audit": {"by": "Sonnet 5.5", "verdict": "clean", "reason": "libs.py, run twice on pasted boards, flood-fills each group and prints its stones, liberty count and a DANGER flag when it has two or fewer liberties. It only inspects the current position, with no candidate moves or look-ahead, and the output is just group listings.", "stop": false}}